retained quality & $0.9790$ & $0.9832$ & $0.9853$ & $0.9862$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent against the gate $0.98$: the knee is $24$, identical to shard A. The shard-level-difference hypothesis is refuted; the grid-artifact hypothesis is confirmed; and the hypothesis that the truth lies strictly between $24$ and $32$ is refuted. The consequence is a complete dual-corpus deployment table: the chain $\{16,20,24\}$ at contexts $\{512,1024,2048\}$ replicates exactly, cell for cell, on two disjoint corpora. Shard B's absolute accuracy is $0.4946$ against shard A's $0.4760$ --- the second text is easier --- yet the knees coincide.

\subsection{What this paper proves}

The empirical episode is the motivation; the content is a general theory of threshold sweeps. Section~\ref{sec:setting} fixes definitions. Section~\ref{sec:fact} proves the factorisation theorem and its corollaries, including an underdetermination result, and instantiates them on the measured data. Section~\ref{sec:repl} proves the replication law, its sharpness, and the scale invariance decoupling accuracy level from threshold position. Section~\ref{sec:chain} records the closed-form logarithmic law satisfied by the measured chain and a trichotomy excluding two standard attention-profile families. Sections~\ref{sec:cap}--\ref{sec:prod} solve the design problem. Section~\ref{sec:algo} gives algorithms and Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses applications, limitations and open directions.

\section{Setting and definitions}\label{sec:setting}

Throughout, budgets are positive integers and quality values are rationals.

\begin{definition}[Quality curve, gate, knee]
A \emph{quality curve} is a function $A:\N\to\Q$; it is \emph{monotone} if $j\le k$ implies $A(j)\le A(k)$. Given a gate $g\in\Q$, the \textbf{knee} is
\[
\kstar(A,g)=\inf\{k\in\N : A(k)\ge g\},
\]
an infimum over naturals, attained whenever the set is nonempty.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Grid knee]
For a set $G\subseteq\N$ of sampled budgets, the \textbf{grid knee} --- the only quantity a sweep can report --- is
\[
\hat{k}(G,A,g)=\inf\{k : k\in G \text{ and } A(k)\ge g\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Localisation]\label{def:loc}
Let $r\ge 1$ be an integer. A finite grid $G\subseteq\N$ \textbf{localises} $[1,N]$ \emph{to a factor $r$} if for every $c\in[1,N]$ there is $g\in G$ with $c\le g\le rc$. This is the \emph{deployment-safe} requirement: the sampled budget is never below the target and overshoots by at most $r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two-sided and asymmetric localisation]\label{def:asym}
$G$ is a \textbf{two-sided} $r$-localiser of $[1,N]$ if for every $c\in[1,N]$ there is $g\in G$ with $g\le rc$ and $c\le rg$. More generally, for integers $a,b\ge1$, $G$ is an \textbf{$(a,b)$-localiser} of $[1,N]$ if
\[
\forall c\in[1,N]\ \exists g\in G:\quad g\le b\,c \ \text{ and } \ c\le a\,g,
\]
i.e.\ $g$ overshoots $c$ by at most $b$ and undershoots it by at most $a$. The case $a=1$, $b=r$ is Definition~\ref{def:loc}; the case $a=b=r$ is the two-sided notion.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric sum]
For integers $r,s\ge0$ put $\Sig(r,s)=\sum_{i=0}^{s-1}r^{\,i+1}=r+r^2+\cdots+r^s$, with $\Sig(r,0)=0$. Two identities are used constantly:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rec}
\Sig(r,s+1)=\Sig(r,s)+r^{s+1},\qquad \Sig(r,s+1)=r\,\Sig(r,s)+r.
\end{equation}
The second says that one more sample point multiplies the reachable range by $r$ and adds $r$. For $r\ge1$, $\Sig(r,\cdot)$ is strictly increasing, and $\Sig(1,s)=s$.
\end{definition}

\section{What a sweep measures: the factorisation theorem}\label{sec:fact}

\begin{theorem}[Factorisation]\label{thm:fact}
Let $A$ be monotone and suppose some $k\in G$ satisfies $A(k)\ge g$. Then
\[
\hat{k}(G,A,g)=\inf\{k : k\in G \text{ and } k\ge \kstar(A,g)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For monotone $A$ with the gate met somewhere, $\kstar(A,g)\le k \iff A(k)\ge g$: the forward direction is monotonicity applied to $A(\kstar)\ge g$, the backward direction is minimality. Substituting this equivalence inside the infimum defining $\hat k$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

The content is that the map (curve) $\mapsto$ (reading) \emph{factors through the single number} $\kstar$. All other information in the curve is annihilated by the sweep.

\begin{corollary}[Sweeps over-report]
$\kstar(A,g)\le\hat{k}(G,A,g)$ whenever the gate is met on $G$. A reported knee is an upper bound: provisioning at it is always safe.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Exactness is grid membership]\label{cor:exact}
$\hat{k}(G,A,g)=\kstar(A,g)$ if and only if $\kstar(A,g)\in G$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Refinement is monotone]\label{cor:refine}
If $G\subseteq H$ and the gate is met on $G$, then $\hat{k}(H,A,g)\le\hat{k}(G,A,g)$: a finer sweep never reports a larger knee.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Bracketing]\label{prop:bracket}
If $A$ is monotone, $A(a)<g$ and $A(b)\ge g$, then $a<\kstar(A,g)\le b$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling-grid artifact bound]
Let $D=\{2^j:j\ge0\}$ and let $A$ be monotone with the gate met somewhere and $\kstar\ge1$. Then $\hat{k}(D,A,g)<2\kstar(A,g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $m$ be minimal with $\kstar\le2^m$. Then $2^m\in D$ and $A(2^m)\ge g$, so $\hat k\le 2^m$; minimality gives $2^{m-1}<\kstar$ for $m\ge1$, whence $2^m<2\kstar$. The case $m=0$ is immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Underdetermination]\label{thm:under}
Fix the coarse grid $C=\{8,16,32,64\}$ and let $a,b\in(16,32]$ be arbitrary. There exist monotone curves $A$, $B$ with $A|_C=B|_C$, $\kstar(A,g)=a$, $\kstar(B,g)=b$ and $\hat{k}(C,A,g)=\hat{k}(C,B,g)=32$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Use step curves $S_c(k)=\mathbf{1}[k\ge c]$, monotone with knee $c$ at any gate in $(0,1]$. For $c\in(16,32]$ all such curves vanish at $8,16$ and equal $1$ at $32,64$, so they agree on $C$ and all give the coarse reading $32$. Take $A=S_a$, $B=S_b$.
\end{proof}

Since $A$ and $B$ agree at every sampled point, they yield identical readings \emph{for every gate}. A coarse reading of $32$ is thus compatible with any true knee in $(16,32]$, and comparing a coarse reading on one corpus with a fine reading on another can never be evidence of a corpus-level difference.

\paragraph{The measured instance.}
Model the empirical row as a curve assembled from nonnegative per-key gains, $A(k)=\sum_{i<k}w_i$, automatically monotone. Choosing the gains to match the measured table gives a curve $B_{\mathrm{obs}}$ with $B_{\mathrm{obs}}(20)=0.9790$, $B_{\mathrm{obs}}(24)=0.9832$, $B_{\mathrm{obs}}(28)=0.9853$, $B_{\mathrm{obs}}(32)=0.9862$, no gain accruing strictly between $20$ and $23$.

\begin{theorem}[The disagreement was a grid artifact]\label{thm:artifact}
With $g=0.98$, $F=\{16,20,24,28,32\}$ and $C=\{8,16,32,64\}$:
\[
\kstar(B_{\mathrm{obs}},g)=24,\quad \hat{k}(F,B_{\mathrm{obs}},g)=24,\quad \hat{k}(C,B_{\mathrm{obs}},g)=32,\quad \hat{k}(C,\cdot)<2\kstar .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$B_{\mathrm{obs}}(24)\ge g$ and $B_{\mathrm{obs}}(k)<g$ for $k\le23$ give the true knee $24$; $24\in F$ and Corollary~\ref{cor:exact} give the fine reading; on $C$ the least point at or above $24$ is $32$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:fact} gives the coarse reading; finally $32<48$.
\end{proof}

One corpus, one curve, two readings.

\section{Replication and the accuracy/knee decoupling}\label{sec:repl}

\begin{theorem}[Replication law]\label{thm:repl}
Let $|A(j)-B(j)|\le\varepsilon$ for all $j$. Suppose $A(k)\ge g+\varepsilon$ and $A(j)+\varepsilon<g$ for all $j<k$. Then $\kstar(B,g)=k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$B(k)\ge A(k)-\varepsilon\ge g$ gives $\kstar(B,g)\le k$; for $j<k$, $B(j)\le A(j)+\varepsilon<g$ excludes smaller budgets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Agreement]
Under the same hypotheses, $\kstar(A,g)=\kstar(B,g)=k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Measured replication threshold]
For $B_{\mathrm{obs}}$ and $g=0.98$, the gate is cleared at $24$ by $0.0032$ and missed at every $k\le23$ by at least $0.0010$. Hence every curve uniformly within $\varepsilon=0.0009$ of $B_{\mathrm{obs}}$ has knee exactly $24$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The margin hypothesis is necessary]
For every $\varepsilon\in(0,1]$ and every $N\ge1$ there are monotone curves $A,B$ with $|A(j)-B(j)|\le\varepsilon$ for all $j$, $\kstar(A,g)=1$ and $\kstar(B,g)=N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Place the curves on opposite sides of the gate throughout $[1,N)$ with vertical separation at most $\varepsilon$: $A$ just above $g$ from budget $1$, $B$ just below $g$ until $N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Retention curve]
Given a raw sweep $\mathrm{raw}:\N\to\Q$ and a context $\mathrm{ctx}$, the retention curve is $R(k)=\mathrm{raw}(k)/\mathrm{raw}(\mathrm{ctx})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Scale invariance]\label{thm:scale}
For every $c\ne0$, every context and every gate, the knee of the retention curve built from $c\cdot\mathrm{raw}$ equals the knee of the one built from $\mathrm{raw}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The two retention curves are equal as functions, since $c\,\mathrm{raw}(k)/(c\,\mathrm{raw}(\mathrm{ctx}))=\mathrm{raw}(k)/\mathrm{raw}(\mathrm{ctx})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If shard B's raw sweep is shard A's multiplied by the difficulty factor $0.4946/0.4760$, the two shards have identical knees at every gate. Absolute accuracy level and knee position are independent coordinates.
\end{corollary}

\section{The measured chain: closed form and profile trichotomy}\label{sec:chain}

The dual-corpus deployment table is $\kstar(512)=16$, $\kstar(1024)=20$, $\kstar(2048)=24$; equivalently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:loglaw}
\kstar(\mathrm{ctx})=4\log_2\mathrm{ctx}-20 .
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Prediction, not fit]
If $9a+b=16$ and $10a+b=20$ then $a=4$, $b=-20$ and $11a+b=24$. Conversely if $9a+b=16$ and $a\ne4$ then $11a+b\ne24$.
\end{proposition}

So the third cell was forced by the first two under the hypothesis of a law affine in $\log_2\mathrm{ctx}$, and any competing slope is discriminated by the measurement --- which returned $24$ on both corpora.

\begin{theorem}[Zipf is too dear]
For the Zipf profile $p_i\propto1/(i+1)$ on $n=2^{11}$ keys and gate $0.98$, the knee exceeds $32$, hence differs from the measured $24$. Moreover for every gate $\tau\in(0,1]$ and every $K$ there is a context whose Zipf knee exceeds $K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Retention is $H_{\min(k,n)}/H_n$. The dyadic bounds $1+m/2\le H_{2^m}\le1+m$ turn the requirement $H_{2^j}\ge\tau H_{2^m}$ into a linear inequality in $j$ that fails for $j$ small relative to $m$, forcing the knee above $2^j$. Instantiating $m=11$, $\tau=0.98$, $j=5$ excludes all budgets up to $32$; letting $m\to\infty$ pushes the knee beyond any $K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Truncated geometric is too cheap and rigid]
For the profile $p_i\propto2^{-i}$ on $n\ge10$ keys and gate $0.98$, the knee is exactly $6$ --- the same at context $512$ and at context $2048$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$1-2^{-5}=0.96875<0.98\le0.984375=1-2^{-6}$, and for $n\ge10$ the normalising denominator lies within $2^{-10}$ of $1$, too small to flip either inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Profile trichotomy]
The measured chain is incompatible with both families: the Zipf knee at context $2048$ exceeds $24$; the geometric knee is context-free; the measured knee satisfies \eqref{eq:loglaw} and moves by $8$ keys between contexts $512$ and $2048$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Exact sweep capacity and rigidity}\label{sec:cap}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:fact}, ``$G$ localises $[1,N]$ to a factor $r$'' is exactly the statement that a sweep on $G$ reports every knee in $[1,N]$ to within a factor $r$.

\begin{proposition}[Packing bound]
If $G$ localises $[1,N]$ to a factor $r\ge1$ then $N<(r+1)^{|G|}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The intervals $[(r+1)^i,\,r(r+1)^i]$ for $i=0,\dots,|G|$ are pairwise disjoint since $r(r+1)^i<(r+1)^{i+1}$. If $N\ge(r+1)^{|G|}$ each contains a budget $\le N$ and hence its own grid point, giving $|G|+1$ distinct points.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, a grid with fewer than $\log_{r+1}N$ points has a \emph{blind budget}. In particular no four-point grid localises $[1,81]$ to a factor $2$. The bound is never tight.

\begin{theorem}[Capacity upper bound]\label{thm:capub}
If $|G|=n$, $r\ge1$, and $G$ localises $[1,N]$ to a factor $r$, then $N\le\Sig(r,n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$; for $n=0$ the empty grid cannot serve $c=1$. For $n\ge1$, $N\ge1$, let $M=\max G$; serving $c=N$ gives $N\le M$. Put $N'=\lfloor(M-1)/r\rfloor$: any $c\le N'$ has $rc\le M-1<M$, so $M$ cannot serve it and $G\setminus\{M\}$ localises $[1,\min(N,N')]$. Induction gives $\min(N,N')\le\Sig(r,n-1)$; in the nontrivial case $N\le M\le rN'+r\le r\Sig(r,n-1)+r=\Sig(r,n)$ by \eqref{eq:rec}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Offset geometric grid]
$\mathcal{G}(r,s)=\{\Sig(r,j+1):0\le j<s\}=\{r,\ r+r^2,\ \dots,\ r+\cdots+r^s\}$, of cardinality $s$ for $r\ge1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]\label{thm:attain}
$\mathcal{G}(r,s)$ localises $[1,\Sig(r,s)]$ to a factor $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Given $1\le c\le\Sig(r,s)$ let $j$ be least with $c\le\Sig(r,j)$, so $j\ge1$ and $\Sig(r,j-1)<c$. Take $g=\Sig(r,j)$: then $c\le g$, and $g=r\Sig(r,j-1)+r\le r(c-1)+r=rc$.
\end{proof}

Each optimal point sits at the exact \emph{top} of the interval it can serve, wasting no reach.

\begin{theorem}[Exact capacity]\label{thm:capexact}
For $r\ge1$ and every $s$, $\max\{N:\exists G,\ |G|=s,\ G \text{ localises } [1,N] \text{ to a factor } r\}=\Sig(r,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Both naive guesses are wrong]
The guess $r^{|G|}$ is too small: $\{2,6\}$ localises $[1,6]$ to a factor $2$ and $6>2^2$. The packing bound is too large: $\Sig(r,s)<(r+1)^s$ for all $r,s\ge1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $r\ge1$, $|G|=s$, and suppose $G$ localises $[1,\Sig(r,s)]$ to a factor $r$. Then $G=\mathcal{G}(r,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct, tracking equality in the peeling argument. Strict monotonicity of $\Sig(r,\cdot)$ forces the case $N'<N$; then $N\le M\le rN'+r\le r\Sig(r,s-1)+r=N$ is an equality chain, giving $N'=\Sig(r,s-1)$ and $M=\Sig(r,s)$. The induction hypothesis identifies $G\setminus\{M\}$ with $\mathcal{G}(r,s-1)$, and $\mathcal{G}(r,s)=\mathcal{G}(r,s-1)\cup\{\Sig(r,s)\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Structure of optima]
An optimal grid contains the top of its range, $\Sig(r,s)\in G$; and for $r\ge2$ it never contains the budget $1$. In particular the classical grid $\{1,r,r^2,\dots\}$ is structurally excluded.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Rigidity is exactly a capacity phenomenon]
Both $\{2,6\}$ and $\{2,5\}$ localise $[1,5]$ to a factor $2$, yet only $\{2,6\}$ localises $[1,6]$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Minimal sweep cost]
For $r\ge1$, the least number of sample points localising $[1,N]$ to a factor $r$ is the least $s$ with $N\le\Sig(r,s)$; such an $s$ exists since $s\le\Sig(r,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The measured instance]
(i) Four doubling-accurate points cover exactly $[1,30]$, and no four-point grid covers $[1,31]$. (ii) Any four-point grid localising $[1,30]$ to a factor $2$ equals $\{2,6,14,30\}$. (iii) The coarse grid $\{8,16,32,64\}$ does not localise $[1,30]$: the budget $3$ has no grid point in $[3,6]$. (iv) Localising $[1,64]$ to a factor $2$ costs exactly six points; the coarse sweep used four.
\end{corollary}

\section{Relaxing the deployment constraint: two-sided sweeps}\label{sec:two}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided capacity]\label{thm:two}
For $r\ge1$, the maximum range coverable by $s$ points with a factor $r$ on either side is exactly $\Sig(r^2,s)=r^2+r^4+\cdots+r^{2s}$, attained by $\mathcal{T}(r,s)=\{r(\Sig(r^2,j)+1):0\le j<s\}$, which is the unique optimum at capacity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $a=b=r$ instance of Theorems~\ref{thm:asymub}, \ref{thm:asymattain} and~\ref{thm:asymrigid}: peeling now loses a factor $r$ at the bottom of the interval a point serves and gains a factor $r$ at the top, so the two compound into $r^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[A natural guess is refuted]
The value $r^{2s-1}$ is strictly too small: at $r=2$, $s=2$ it predicts $8$, while $\mathcal{T}(2,2)=\{2,10\}$ genuinely covers $[1,20]$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The price of never under-provisioning]
For $r\ge2$ and $s\ge1$, $\Sig(r,2s-1)<\Sig(r^2,s)<\Sig(r,2s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Left: the single top term $r^{2s}$ of the two-sided sum already exceeds $\Sig(r,2s-1)$, since $\Sig(r,m)+r\le r^{m+1}$ for $r\ge2$. Right: induction matches the terms of $\Sig(r^2,s)$ with the even-indexed terms of $\Sig(r,2s)$, the odd-indexed ones remaining as strict slack.
\end{proof}

So an $s$-point two-sided sweep beats a $(2s-1)$-point one-sided sweep and loses to a $2s$-point one: the deployment constraint costs a factor two in sample points, and no more. At $r=2$, $s=4$: two-sided capacity $340$ attained uniquely by $\{2,10,42,170\}$, one-sided capacity $30$ attained uniquely by $\{2,6,14,30\}$.

\section{The product law}\label{sec:prod}

\begin{theorem}[Asymmetric capacity, upper bound]\label{thm:asymub}
If $|G|=n$, $a,b\ge1$, and $G$ is an $(a,b)$-localiser of $[1,N]$, then $N\le\Sig(ab,n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct, peeling $M=\max G$. Serving $c=N$ gives $N\le aM$. Put $N'=\lfloor(M-1)/b\rfloor$; any $c\le N'$ has $bc\le M-1<M$, so $M$ cannot serve it and $G\setminus\{M\}$ is an $(a,b)$-localiser of $[1,\min(N,N')]$. Induction gives $\min(N,N')\le\Sig(ab,n-1)$, and in the nontrivial case
\[
N\le aM\le a(bN'+b)=ab\,N'+ab\le ab\,\Sig(ab,n-1)+ab=\Sig(ab,n).
\]
The tolerances enter as a product because one governs the bottom of the interval a point serves and the other its top.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Asymmetric grid]
$\mathcal{A}(a,b,s)=\{b(\Sig(ab,j)+1):0\le j<s\}$, of cardinality $s$ for $a,b\ge1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]\label{thm:asymattain}
$\mathcal{A}(a,b,s)$ is an $(a,b)$-localiser of $[1,\Sig(ab,s)]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Given $1\le c\le\Sig(ab,s)$ let $j$ be least with $c\le\Sig(ab,j)$, so $j\ge1$ and $\Sig(ab,j-1)<c$. Take $g=b(\Sig(ab,j-1)+1)$: then $g\le bc$, and $ag=ab\Sig(ab,j-1)+ab=\Sig(ab,j)\ge c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact asymmetric capacity]
For $a,b\ge1$ and every $s$, $\max\{N:\exists G,\ |G|=s,\ G \text{ an } (a,b)\text{-localiser of } [1,N]\}=\Sig(ab,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Asymmetric rigidity]\label{thm:asymrigid}
If $|G|=s$ and $G$ is an $(a,b)$-localiser of $[1,\Sig(ab,s)]$, then $G=\mathcal{A}(a,b,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The peeling chain $N\le aM\le a(bN'+b)\le ab\Sig(ab,s-1)+ab=N$ collapses to equalities, forcing $N'=\Sig(ab,s-1)$ and $M=b(\Sig(ab,s-1)+1)$; induction identifies the rest, and $\mathcal{A}(a,b,s)=\mathcal{A}(a,b,s-1)\cup\{b(\Sig(ab,s-1)+1)\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Product law]\label{thm:productlaw}
Let $a,b,a',b'\ge1$ with $ab=a'b'$. Then for every $s$,
\[
\{N:\exists G,\,|G|=s,\,G \text{ an } (a,b)\text{-localiser of }[1,N]\}
=\{N:\exists G,\,|G|=s,\,G \text{ an } (a',b')\text{-localiser of }[1,N]\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sets are the initial segment $[0,\Sig(ab,s)]=[0,\Sig(a'b',s)]$: membership is bounded by Theorem~\ref{thm:asymub}, and every $N$ below capacity is achieved by the corresponding asymmetric grid, localisation restricting to subranges.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The earlier theorems are instances]
$(a,b)=(1,r)$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:capexact} with capacity $\Sig(r,s)$ and $\mathcal{A}(1,r,s)=\mathcal{G}(r,s)$ (this identity is the recurrence $r(\Sig(r,j)+1)=\Sig(r,j+1)$); $(a,b)=(r,r)$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:two} with capacity $\Sig(r^2,s)$ and $\mathcal{A}(r,r,s)=\mathcal{T}(r,s)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The tolerance trade]
With four sample points, tolerance $(1,2)$ localises $[1,30]$, tolerance $(2,2)$ localises $[1,340]$, and tolerance $(1,4)$ localises $[1,340]$. Doubling the one-sided tolerance is worth exactly as much as symmetrising it; only the product matters.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Sweep reading with certificate.} Sort $G$ and return the least $k\in G$ with $A(k)\ge g$ together with the bracketing cell of Proposition~\ref{prop:bracket}. Cost $O(|G|\log|G|)$; the cell is the sharpest statement the data support.

\paragraph{Optimal grid construction.} Given $a,b\ge1$ and a target $N$, take $s$ least with $N\le\Sig(ab,s)$ and emit $\mathcal{A}(a,b,s)$. Cost $O(s)$ with $s=O(\log_{ab}N)$ for $ab\ge2$; optimal by Theorem~\ref{thm:asymrigid}, uniquely so at capacity.

\paragraph{Replication certificate.} Compute $\varepsilon^{*}=\min\big(A(k)-g,\ \min_{j<k}(g-A(j))\big)$ and report that any curve uniformly within $\varepsilon<\varepsilon^{*}$ of $A$ has knee exactly $k$. For the measured row $\varepsilon^{*}=0.0010$, so $\varepsilon=0.0009$ certifies.

\paragraph{Exhaustive verification.} For small parameters, enumerate all $s$-subsets of $[1,N_{\max}]$ and test localisation directly; this reproduces $\Sig(2,3)=14$ with unique optimum $\{2,6,14\}$ and $\Sig(4,2)=20$ with unique optimum $\{2,10\}$. Cost $\binom{N_{\max}}{s}\cdot O(sN)$ --- feasible only for toy sizes, which is why the closed form matters.

\section{Discussion and future directions}\label{sec:disc}

\paragraph{Methodological consequences.} Whenever a threshold is estimated by sampling a monotone response on a grid, the estimate is the ceiling of the truth in the grid and nothing more. Comparisons across studies are meaningful only at equal grid resolution; a coarse/fine disagreement is always resolved in favour of the fine sweep (Corollary~\ref{cor:refine}) and needs no substantive explanation; reporting the bracketing cell instead of a point estimate is strictly more informative and free; and reporting the margins at the bracketing samples converts a point measurement into a replication guarantee with an explicit tolerance (Theorem~\ref{thm:repl}), which the necessity result shows cannot be weakened.

\paragraph{Design consequences.} The reflexive grid $\{1,r,r^2,\dots\}$ is not merely suboptimal but structurally excluded for $r\ge2$: sampling the very bottom wastes a point whose reach is only $[1,r]$. Conversely, starting a doubling sweep high --- as in $\{8,16,32,64\}$ --- creates blindness at the bottom of the range. And the deployment constraint is cheap: it costs a factor two in points, and can be bought back exactly by squaring the overshoot tolerance.

\paragraph{Scope.} The theory assumes monotone curves and integer tolerances; noise is handled through the margin machinery, Theorem~\ref{thm:repl} being readable as a sup-norm robustness statement. The empirical claims concern one model family, one gate, three contexts and two text corpora.

\paragraph{Future directions.} (1) Domain-jump corpora (code, mathematics) to test whether the chain is architectural or distributional. (2) Larger models and quantised-offload configurations, to test whether the slope of four keys per context doubling is size dependent. (3) Real-valued and continuous versions of the capacity theorems. (4) The exact adaptive/non-adaptive gap: a sequential sweep is a bisection needing $\Theta(\log N)$ points, and the gap at tolerance $(a,b)$ is unquantified. (5) Noisy capacity: the resolving power of $s$ points when each evaluation is corrupted by noise bounded by $\varepsilon$. (6) Identification of the attention-profile families whose knee grows logarithmically in the context, filling the gap between the excluded Zipf and geometric extremes.

\end{document}

